### 2. Core Concepts: Why Each Component is Designed This Way

```
src/extract/
├── __init__.py
├── spotify_client.py     ──▶ [1] Token Manager + 429 Rate-Limit Retry Engine
├── artist_extractor.py   ──▶ [2] Artist Search & Canonical ID Resolver
├── album_extractor.py    ──▶ [3] Discography Paginator (limit=10)
├── track_extractor.py    ──▶ [4] Album Track Paginator (limit=10)
└── main.py               ──▶ [5] Master Orchestrator (Writes to data/raw/)
```

| Component | Responsibility | Why We Design It This Way |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`spotify_client.py`** | Token Caching + Resilient HTTP | Spotify tokens expire in 1 hour (3600s). Re-authenticating on every request triggers rate limits. We cache the token with a 60s safety buffer. When hit with HTTP 429, we parse the `Retry-After` header and back off with exponential jitter. |
| **`artist_extractor.py`** | Search & Canonical Entity Resolution | Takes natural artist names (e.g. "Taylor Swift"), resolves their canonical `artist_id`, extracts high-res image URLs, Spotify URI, genres, and attaches `extracted_at`. |
| **`album_extractor.py`** | Discography Paginator | Spotify's 2026 API limits album page size to `limit=10`. We implement a strict offset paginator (`offset += len(items)`) covering Studio Albums, Singles, and Compilations, deduplicating album IDs. |
| **`track_extractor.py`** | Track Paginator | Paginates through `/v1/albums/{id}/tracks` using `limit=10`. Traverses all tracks and captures duration, explicit flag, track number, disc number, and foreign keys (`album_id`, `artist_id`). |
| **`main.py`** | Master Orchestration & Raw Storage | Coordinates the 8 target artists, injects the critical temporal audit field **`snapshot_date`** (`YYYY-MM-DD`), and writes partitioned, immutable JSON payloads to `data/raw/artists/`, `data/raw/albums/`, `data/raw/tracks/`. |

